const e={intro:["Behind every canon list stands a stack of parchment. Until the nineteenth century the printed Greek New Testament rested on late medieval minuscules — copies of copies, none older than the tenth century. Then four books written between roughly 330 and 450 came into scholarly hands within a single generation, and the text of the Bible had to be argued again from the beginning.","They are called *uncials* or *majuscules* after their script: unjoined capitals, no word division, no punctuation to speak of, written continuously across the page. They are called *codices* because they are books rather than scrolls — the Christians adopted the codex early and almost universally, and these are the form at its most ambitious: whole Bibles, Old Testament and New, between two covers, at a cost in parchment that only an imperial or episcopal purse could meet.","What makes them dangerous to any tidy account of the canon is not what they omit but what they include. **Sinaiticus** copies the *Epistle of Barnabas* and the *Shepherd of Hermas* straight on after Revelation. **Alexandrinus** adds *1 Clement* and *2 Clement*, and its index promises the *Psalms of Solomon*. These were not appendices in the modern sense; they were bound in, in the same hand, in the same order of reading. The fourth-century church that produced the oldest complete Bibles we have was not yet holding the book that any church holds now."],groups:[{key:"greek-uncials",label:"The Great Greek Uncials",blurb:"Whole Bibles in Greek capitals, written between roughly 330 and 450 on parchment that cost a herd of animals. Four of them arrived in scholarly hands within a single generation and the text of the New Testament had to be argued again from the beginning. Two more, later and stranger, are here because each carries something no other book on earth has."},{key:"papyri",label:"Older Than Any Codex",blurb:"Fragments, not Bibles — a leaf, a gathering, a bundle of Paul. But they are one to two centuries older than the uncials, they came out of the Egyptian sand in the twentieth century, and one of them settled an argument that had run since 1881."},{key:"latin",label:"The Latin Line",blurb:"For a thousand years the West did not read the Greek. It read Jerome — and before Jerome a scatter of Old Latin versions he was sent to tidy up. This is that line: the text, the argument it started, and the manuscripts that carry it, including the heaviest book in this portal."},{key:"versions",label:"The Other Languages",blurb:"Scripture went east and south as fast as it went west, and the manuscripts that carry it in Syriac, Gothic and Geʼez are witnesses to the Greek behind them — sometimes to a Greek older than any surviving Greek copy."}],entries:[{slug:"vaticanus",group:"greek-uncials",columns:"three",longEnding:!1,extras:"nothing — but it stops at Hebrews 9:14",city:"Rome",siglum:"B",ga:"GA 03",name:"Codex Vaticanus",native:"Vaticanus graecus 1209",date:"c. 300–350 CE",place:"Egypt or Caesarea; unresolved",script:"Greek majuscule, three columns to the page — the only one of the four so ruled",home:"Biblioteca Apostolica Vaticana, Rome (Vat. gr. 1209)",exposition:["The oldest substantially complete Bible in existence, and the least travelled: Vaticanus appears in the Vatican Library’s first surviving catalogue, drawn up in **1475**, and where it was before that nobody knows. It has sat on essentially the same shelf ever since, which is why it is the best preserved of the four and why, for three centuries, it was also the least usable.","Its text is the reason the modern critical editions look as they do. Where the medieval manuscripts had accumulated harmonizations — a phrase from Matthew quietly repaired into Mark, a smoother reading displacing a rougher one — Vaticanus is consistently shorter and harder. **Westcott and Hort** built their 1881 edition on it and on Sinaiticus, and the *Nestle-Aland* text that stands behind almost every modern translation is their descendant.","It is damaged at both ends and in the middle. Genesis 1:1–46:28 is gone, Psalms 105–137 are gone, and the New Testament stops in the middle of a sentence at **Hebrews 9:14** — so the Pastoral Epistles, Philemon and Revelation are simply absent, supplied by a fifteenth-century hand on replacement leaves. Whether the original scribe wrote them and the quires were lost, or never wrote them at all, cannot be settled from the book.","Down its margins run pairs of horizontal dots, the *distigmai*, beside places where the text was known to vary. Someone was collating this manuscript against another, marking disagreements. When they did it is disputed — a fourth-century hand would make the marks the earliest surviving critical apparatus in Christian history; a sixteenth-century one would make them a Renaissance scholar’s notes."],contains:"Most of the Septuagint; the New Testament to Hebrews 9:14. No Pastorals, no Philemon, no Revelation in the original hand. The deuterocanonical books stand in their Greek places without distinction — there is no separate section for them, because for this scribe there was no separate category.",markEnding:"Mark ends at **16:8** — “for they were afraid.” The scribe left the rest of the column and the whole of the next column blank, the only such gap in the New Testament portion of the book. Something was known to be missing, or known to be disputed.",access:"Access was restricted for most of its documented life. Tischendorf was permitted a few hours in 1866 and copied what he could from memory between sessions; usable photographic facsimiles arrived only in 1889–1890. The full colour digitization went online in 2015.",proves:"That a fourth-century Bible could be assembled without the books a later Bible would call essential — and that shortness, not fullness, is usually the older reading.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Vatican City, Biblioteca Apostolica Vaticana, Vat. gr. 1209 — full colour digitisation, DigiVatLib",note:"The book itself: the blank column after Mark 16:8 and the distigmai down the margins can be looked at rather than taken on report."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Bibliorum Sacrorum Graecus Codex Vaticanus, photographic facsimile ed. Giuseppe Cozza-Luzi (Rome, 1889–1890)",note:"The facsimile that ended three centuries of scholars working from other people's collations."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Westcott & Hort, The New Testament in the Original Greek (Cambridge, 1881)",note:"The edition built on this manuscript; the Introduction states the reasoning every later editor inherited or argued with."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Bruce M. Metzger & Bart D. Ehrman, The Text of the New Testament: Its Transmission, Corruption, and Restoration, 4th ed. (Oxford, 2005)",note:"The standard handbook — its description of B is the baseline account, and its notes are where the further literature is."},{kind:"study",ref:"Patrick Andrist (ed.), Le manuscrit B de la Bible (Vaticanus graecus 1209) (Lausanne, 2009)",note:"Studies of the codex as a physical object — quires, ruling, correctors — including the argument over the marginal marks."},{kind:"study",ref:'Philip B. Payne, "Fuldensis, Sigla for Variants in Vaticanus, and 1 Cor 14.34–5," New Testament Studies 41 (1995)',note:"Argues the distigmai are an early text-critical apparatus; the early dating has been disputed since, and the question is open."}]},{slug:"sinaiticus",group:"greek-uncials",columns:"four",longEnding:!1,extras:"Barnabas; Shepherd of Hermas",city:"London, Leipzig, St Petersburg, Sinai",siglum:"ℵ",ga:"GA 01",name:"Codex Sinaiticus",native:"Σιναϊτικός",date:"c. 330–360 CE",place:"Caesarea, Alexandria or Rome; argued in all three directions",script:"Greek majuscule, four columns to the page — an open spread showing eight, closer to a scroll unrolled than to any later book",home:"Split four ways: British Library, London (347 leaves) · Leipzig University Library (43) · National Library of Russia, St Petersburg (fragments) · St Catherine’s Monastery, Sinai (leaves found in a blocked room in 1975)",exposition:["The only one of the four that carries a **complete Greek New Testament**, and the most contested object in biblical scholarship. **Constantin von Tischendorf** first saw leaves of it at St Catherine’s Monastery beneath Mount Sinai in 1844 and left with forty-three of them, which are in Leipzig. He returned in 1853 and found nothing. He returned again in **1859**, under Russian patronage, and this time the steward produced the bulk of the manuscript wrapped in a red cloth.","What happened next is not agreed. Tischendorf’s account is that the monastery presented the codex to **Tsar Alexander II** as protector of the Orthodox Church; a formal deed of gift was signed in 1869, ten years after the book left. The monastery has maintained since the nineteenth century that the manuscript was **lent** for copying and never returned. Both positions are on the record; the 1975 discovery of further leaves still at Sinai returned the question to the table rather than settling it.","The Soviet government, needing currency and having no use for a Christian Bible, sold the Petersburg portion to the British Museum in **1933** for £100,000 — raised in part by public subscription, sixpences included. It remains the largest sum ever paid for a book at the time it was paid.","Three scribes wrote it and correctors worked over it for centuries afterward — the manuscript carries some **27,000 corrections**, more than any comparable book. It is not a clean copy; it is a working Bible that a community argued with, in the margins, for six hundred years."],contains:"About half the Septuagint surviving, the whole New Testament, and then — with no break, no heading, nothing to mark a change of status — the *Epistle of Barnabas* and part of the *Shepherd of Hermas*.",markEnding:"Mark ends at **16:8**, as in Vaticanus. John 7:53–8:11, the woman taken in adultery, is not in it. The two oldest Bibles in the world agree in lacking two of the most familiar passages in the Gospels.",access:"The four holdings were digitally reunited at codexsinaiticus.org in 2009 — the first time since 1844 that the whole book could be read as one object, by anyone, at no cost.",proves:"That the canon was still open at the edges in the fourth century: whoever commissioned this book expected Barnabas and Hermas to be read in church alongside Revelation.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Codex Sinaiticus Project, codexsinaiticus.org",note:"The four holdings photographed and reunited digitally, with transcription and translation alongside — the only way to read the book whole."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Constantin von Tischendorf, Bibliorum Codex Sinaiticus Petropolitanus (St Petersburg, 1862)",note:"The editio princeps, produced under Russian patronage and part of the acquisition record as well as the textual one."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Bruce M. Metzger & Bart D. Ehrman, The Text of the New Testament, 4th ed. (Oxford, 2005)",note:"The standard handbook account of the manuscript and of Tischendorf's three visits."},{kind:"study",ref:"D. C. Parker, Codex Sinaiticus: The Story of the World's Oldest Bible (London: British Library, 2010)",note:"The current full-length account; sets out the gift-or-loan dispute with the documents rather than adjudicating it in advance."},{kind:"study",ref:"H. J. M. Milne & T. C. Skeat, Scribes and Correctors of the Codex Sinaiticus (London: British Museum, 1938)",note:"Where the scribal hands were distinguished and the corrections counted; still the basis for anything said about either."},{kind:"study",ref:"The Collected Biblical Writings of T. C. Skeat, ed. J. K. Elliott (Leiden: Brill, 2004)",note:"Gathers Skeat's papers on the manuscript's history, including the 1933 sale, from inside the institution that bought it."}]},{slug:"alexandrinus",group:"greek-uncials",columns:"two",longEnding:!0,extras:"1 Clement; 2 Clement; Psalms of Solomon (indexed, lost)",city:"London",siglum:"A",ga:"GA 02",name:"Codex Alexandrinus",native:"Ἀλεξανδρῖνος",date:"c. 400–440 CE",place:"Alexandria, on the tradition its name records and its text supports",script:"Greek majuscule, two columns to the page, with enlarged initials standing out into the margin — a decorative habit the older two do not have",home:"British Library, London (Royal MS 1 D V–VIII)",exposition:["The first of the four to reach Western scholarship, and it arrived as a diplomatic gift. **Cyril Lucaris**, Patriarch of Alexandria and afterwards of Constantinople, sent it to **Charles I** through the English ambassador Sir Thomas Roe; it reached London in **1627**. The King James translators had finished their work sixteen years earlier. Had it come a generation sooner, the most influential English Bible ever printed would have been made from a different Greek text.","Its usefulness was immediate and structural: Alexandrinus was the first great manuscript to be collated into a printed apparatus, in Brian Walton’s **London Polyglot of 1657**, and for nearly two centuries afterward it was simply *the* ancient witness — the standard against which everything else was measured, until Tischendorf’s finds put it third in age.","It taught scholarship a lesson it has never unlearned. Its Gospels are **Byzantine** — the fuller, smoother, later text — while its Acts and Epistles are **Alexandrian**, agreeing closely with Vaticanus. A single book, written by a single team, carries two different textual traditions in two different halves. After Alexandrinus nobody could speak of a manuscript as simply good or bad; you had to say *where* in the manuscript."],contains:"Nearly the entire Septuagint, including **3 and 4 Maccabees**, the fourteen Odes, and Athanasius’ letter on the Psalms; the New Testament nearly entire, followed by **1 Clement** and part of **2 Clement**. Its table of contents also lists the *Psalms of Solomon* at the end — the leaves that held them are lost, but the index proves they were bound in.",markEnding:"Mark carries the **long ending**, 16:9–20 — the earliest surviving Greek manuscript that has it. The gap between it and the two fourth-century codices is where the whole argument about that passage lives.",access:"Damaged at the front: Matthew begins at 25:6, and there are further losses in John and 2 Corinthians. It has been publicly consulted in London since 1753, when the Royal Library passed to the new British Museum.",proves:"That the same scriptorium could hold two texts at once — and that 1 Clement was still being copied as scripture in the fifth century, three hundred years after it was written.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"London, British Library, Royal MS 1 D V–VIII, digitised",note:"The manuscript, including the table of contents that lists the Psalms of Solomon whose leaves are gone."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Frederic G. Kenyon (ed.), The Codex Alexandrinus in Reduced Photographic Facsimile (London: British Museum, 1909–1936)",note:"The complete facsimile, Old Testament and New, on which most twentieth-century work on the codex rests."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Brian Walton (ed.), Biblia Sacra Polyglotta (London, 1654–1657)",note:"Where its readings first entered a printed apparatus — the beginning of collation as a public procedure."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Bruce M. Metzger & Bart D. Ehrman, The Text of the New Testament, 4th ed. (Oxford, 2005)",note:"States the split character of the manuscript — Byzantine Gospels, Alexandrian Acts and Epistles — and what followed methodologically."},{kind:"study",ref:'Scot McKendrick, "The Codex Alexandrinus, or the Dangers of Being a Named Manuscript," in The Bible as Book: The Transmission of the Greek Text (London: British Library, 2003)',note:"On the codex as an object with a curatorial and diplomatic history, not only a textual one."}]},{slug:"ephraemi-rescriptus",group:"greek-uncials",columns:"one",longEnding:!0,extras:"nothing — a palimpsest, two-thirds lost",city:"Paris",siglum:"C",ga:"GA 04",name:"Codex Ephraemi Rescriptus",native:"Codex Ephraemi Syri rescriptus",date:"c. 450 CE, erased in the 12th century",place:"Written in the East, probably Egypt; overwritten at Constantinople",script:"Greek majuscule, a single wide column — then scraped away and rewritten across the grain in the twelfth century",home:"Bibliothèque nationale de France, Paris (Grec 9)",exposition:["A **palimpsest**: a book that was destroyed to make another book. In the twelfth century someone took a fifth-century Bible, scraped the ink from the parchment, turned the leaves, and wrote across them thirty-eight treatises of **Ephrem the Syrian** in Greek translation. Parchment was expensive; sermons were wanted; scripture, evidently, was not scarce. The scraping was imperfect, and the older writing stayed faintly under the newer.","It travelled by inheritance and marriage. The manuscript was in the Medici library in Florence, came to France with **Catherine de’ Medici**, and passed into the royal collection that became the Bibliothèque nationale — which is how a fifth-century Egyptian Bible ended up in Paris under a Syrian ascetic’s homilies.","Reading it was **Tischendorf’s** making. Others had tried and given up; he applied chemical reagents, worked at the faint undertext for two years, and published the New Testament portion in **1843** and the Old Testament in **1845**. He was not yet thirty. The reputation that transcription earned him is what secured the patronage that took him to Sinai — so the fourth-century codex was found because a fifth-century one had been read.","The reagents that revealed it also stained and weakened the parchment, and the damage is permanent. Modern multispectral imaging now recovers erased text without touching it, and much of what Tischendorf could only guess at has since been read clean — a quiet vindication and correction of him at once."],contains:"About two-thirds of the New Testament in fragments — every book except **2 Thessalonians** and **2 John** is represented somewhere — and portions of the Old Testament, chiefly the wisdom books. Nothing is complete; the book survives as a scatter.",markEnding:"Carries the **long ending** of Mark, with Alexandrinus.",access:"The undertext has been re-read with multispectral imaging in the present century, correcting a number of Tischendorf’s readings that stood unchallenged for a hundred and fifty years.",proves:"That the record is recoverable even when it has been deliberately erased — and that the erasers were churchmen, who did not think they were destroying anything irreplaceable.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Paris, Bibliothèque nationale de France, Grec 9, digitised on Gallica",note:"The palimpsest as it now is, with the twelfth-century Ephrem uppermost and the fifth-century Bible showing through it."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Constantin von Tischendorf, Codex Ephraemi Syri rescriptus (Leipzig: New Testament 1843; Old Testament 1845)",note:"The transcription that made Tischendorf's name and secured the patronage that took him to Sinai."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Bruce M. Metzger & Bart D. Ehrman, The Text of the New Testament, 4th ed. (Oxford, 2005)",note:"The standard description of C, its losses, and its mixed textual character."},{kind:"study",ref:'Robert W. Lyon, "A Re-examination of Codex Ephraemi Rescriptus," New Testament Studies',note:"The mid-century check on Tischendorf's readings, done before imaging could settle anything; a number of his transcriptions did not hold."},{kind:"study",ref:"D. C. Parker, An Introduction to the New Testament Manuscripts and their Texts (Cambridge, 2008)",note:"Sets palimpsests and their recovery inside the wider question of what a manuscript witness is."}]},{slug:"bezae",group:"greek-uncials",siglum:"D",ga:"GA 05",name:"Codex Bezae",native:"Codex Bezae Cantabrigiensis",columns:"one",longEnding:!0,extras:"a text of Acts a tenth longer than anyone else’s",city:"Cambridge",date:"c. 400–450 CE",place:"Argued: Berytus, southern Gaul, Egypt, north Africa — genuinely unsettled",script:"Greek and Latin on facing pages, one wide column each, written in sense-lines so that a phrase ends where a reader would pause",home:"Cambridge University Library (MS Nn.2.41)",exposition:["A bilingual: Greek on the left page, Latin on the right, laid out line for line so the eye can cross between them. It contains the four Gospels in the so-called **Western order** — Matthew, John, Luke, Mark — with Acts, and a scrap of 3 John. Nothing else survives.","Its Acts is the scandal and the prize. Where every other witness gives one text, Bezae gives one roughly **a tenth longer**: extra names, extra motives, extra travel details, whole clauses that explain what the shorter text leaves bare. Either a later hand expanded Acts with considerable narrative skill, or Luke issued it twice and this is the other edition. The argument is not settled and has been running since the seventeenth century.","It also contains sentences that exist nowhere else. At Luke 6:5 it inserts an entire scene: Jesus sees a man working on the Sabbath and says that if he knows what he is doing he is blessed, and if he does not he is accursed and a transgressor of the law. One manuscript, one sentence, and a saying of Jesus that no canon has ever carried.","**Theodore Beza** — Calvin’s successor at Geneva — gave it to Cambridge in 1581, having taken it from the monastery of St Irenaeus at Lyon after the city was sacked in 1562. He noted its readings were odd and largely declined to use them."],contains:"The four Gospels in the order Matthew, John, Luke, Mark; Acts; a fragment of 3 John. Greek and Latin throughout, the Latin not simply a translation of the facing Greek.",markEnding:"Carries the **long ending** of Mark.",access:"At Cambridge since 1581 and digitised in full; the standard study is still the two-volume analysis by David Parker (1992).",proves:"That a first-rank ancient manuscript can disagree with every other first-rank ancient manuscript about the length of a whole book — and that “the text of Acts” is a decision, not a given.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Cambridge University Library, MS Nn.2.41, digitised in full",note:"Greek and Latin facing, in sense-lines — the layout is part of the evidence and only visible in the object."},{kind:"primary",ref:"F. H. Scrivener (ed.), Bezae Codex Cantabrigiensis (Cambridge, 1864)",note:"The nineteenth-century edition with the Latin, the correctors' hands, and the readings found nowhere else set out."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Bruce M. Metzger & Bart D. Ehrman, The Text of the New Testament, 4th ed. (Oxford, 2005)",note:"The compact statement of the Western text problem, including the two-editions-of-Acts proposal and the objections to it."},{kind:"study",ref:"D. C. Parker, Codex Bezae: An Early Christian Manuscript and its Text (Cambridge, 1992)",note:"The standard study; treats the book as a made object with a place and date rather than as a bag of variants."},{kind:"study",ref:"D. C. Parker & C.-B. Amphoux (eds.), Codex Bezae: Studies from the Lunel Colloquium (Leiden: Brill, 1996)",note:"Where the disagreements about origin and about the longer Acts are argued between the people holding them."}]},{slug:"washingtonianus",group:"greek-uncials",siglum:"W",ga:"GA 032",name:"Codex Washingtonianus",native:"The Freer Gospels",columns:"one",longEnding:!0,extras:"the Freer Logion — a passage found in no other manuscript",city:"Washington, D.C.",date:"c. 400–450 CE",place:"Egypt; bought at Giza",script:"Greek majuscule, one column, in a sloping hand — and bound between painted wooden covers that are themselves among the oldest icons in existence",home:"Freer Gallery of Art, Smithsonian Institution, Washington, D.C.",exposition:["Bought from a dealer in Giza in **1906** by the Detroit industrialist Charles Lang Freer, who was collecting Asian art and acquired a fourth- or fifth-century Bible almost incidentally. It contains the four Gospels, again in the Western order, and it changes its textual character from book to book as though assembled from whatever exemplars were to hand.","Inside the long ending of Mark, between verses 14 and 15, it carries a passage that exists in **no other Greek manuscript**: the disciples excuse themselves, saying this age of lawlessness is under Satan, who will not let the truth be understood, and asking Christ to reveal his righteousness now. Christ answers that the limit of Satan’s years is fulfilled, but other terrible things draw near.","The passage is called the **Freer Logion**, and it is not quite unattested elsewhere: **Jerome** appears to have known something like it, since he reports a similar addition in Greek copies. So a manuscript bought by accident in Egypt in 1906 turned out to confirm a remark Jerome made around 415 that nobody had been able to check."],contains:"The four Gospels, in the order Matthew, John, Luke, Mark, essentially complete.",markEnding:"The **long ending**, and inside it the Freer Logion — an expansion between 16:14 and 16:15 found nowhere else.",access:"Displayed and digitised by the Freer; Sanders published the text in 1912.",proves:"That the ending of Mark was still being written in the fifth century — and that a patristic remark can wait fifteen hundred years for its manuscript.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Henry A. Sanders, The New Testament Manuscripts in the Freer Collection, Part I: The Washington Manuscript of the Four Gospels (New York, 1912)",note:"The editio princeps, published six years after Freer bought the book at Giza."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Jerome, Dialogus adversus Pelagianos II.15",note:"The remark about an addition in certain Greek copies of Mark — the patristic notice that the Freer Logion turned out to answer."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Bruce M. Metzger & Bart D. Ehrman, The Text of the New Testament, 4th ed. (Oxford, 2005)",note:"Prints and discusses the Freer Logion, and places W among the endings of Mark."},{kind:"study",ref:"Larry W. Hurtado, Text-Critical Methodology and the Pre-Caesarean Text: Codex W in the Gospel of Mark (Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1981)",note:"The book that examined W's shifting textual character rather than assuming a single affiliation for it."},{kind:"study",ref:"Larry W. Hurtado (ed.), The Freer Biblical Manuscripts: Fresh Studies of an American Treasure Trove (Atlanta: SBL, 2006)",note:"Current work on the Freer collection, including the painted covers and the manuscript's Egyptian context."}]},{slug:"p52",group:"papyri",siglum:"𝔓⁵²",ga:"GA P52",name:"The Rylands Fragment",native:"P.Ryl. Gr. 457",date:"c. 125–200 CE",place:"Egypt; acquired at Fayum",script:"Greek majuscule on papyrus, written on both sides — which proves it came from a codex, not a scroll",home:"John Rylands Library, Manchester",exposition:["A scrap the size of a credit card, carrying a few words of **John 18** on each side — Pilate and the question about truth. It sat unread in a box of Egyptian papyri in Manchester until C. H. Roberts identified it in 1934.","It is normally called the oldest surviving fragment of the New Testament, and the claim rests entirely on **handwriting**. There is no date on it; palaeographers compare letter forms with dated documents and estimate. The old confident date of “about 125” has been pulled wider by more recent work, and a range of roughly 125–200 is the honest statement. Even at the late end it is a century older than any codex in this section.","Its most useful feature is not its age but its **shape**. Text on both sides means a leaf of a book, not a column of a roll — physical evidence that Christians had adopted the codex remarkably early, at a time when the rest of the literary world was still unrolling scrolls."],contains:"John 18:31–33 on one side, 18:37–38 on the other. Nothing else.",access:"On display in Manchester and digitised; the dating debate is best followed through Brent Nongbri’s work on the palaeographical method.",proves:"That John was circulating in Egypt within a century of being written — and that the Christian book was a codex almost from the start.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"C. H. Roberts, An Unpublished Fragment of the Fourth Gospel in the John Rylands Library (Manchester University Press, 1935)",note:"The editio princeps, and the source of the confident early date that has been argued about ever since."},{kind:"primary",ref:"P.Ryl. Gr. 457, John Rylands Library, Manchester — digitised",note:"Both sides photographed, which is the whole of the codex argument in one image."},{kind:"study",ref:'Brent Nongbri, "The Use and Abuse of P52: Papyrological Pitfalls in the Dating of the Fourth Gospel," Harvard Theological Review 98 (2005)',note:"Shows that the comparanda permit a range of a century or more; the article that widened the date."},{kind:"study",ref:"Brent Nongbri, God's Library: The Archaeology of the Earliest Christian Manuscripts (New Haven: Yale, 2018)",note:"On what palaeographical dating can and cannot deliver, with the acquisition histories that are usually left out."},{kind:"study",ref:"C. H. Roberts & T. C. Skeat, The Birth of the Codex (Oxford, 1983)",note:"The case that Christians adopted the codex early and near-universally — the claim this fragment is used to support."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Bruce M. Metzger & Bart D. Ehrman, The Text of the New Testament, 4th ed. (Oxford, 2005)",note:"The handbook entry, retaining the older date; useful for seeing what the consensus was before Nongbri."}]},{slug:"p46",group:"papyri",siglum:"𝔓⁴⁶",ga:"GA P46",name:"The Chester Beatty Paul",native:"P. Chester Beatty II / P. Mich. inv. 6238",date:"c. 175–225 CE",place:"Egypt",script:"Greek majuscule on papyrus, one column, in a single-quire codex — every sheet stacked and folded once, so the scribe had to know the total length before starting",home:"Split: Chester Beatty Library, Dublin, and the University of Michigan",exposition:["The earliest substantial copy of **Paul’s letters** — around a century older than Vaticanus, and eighty-six leaves of it survive. Bought in the 1930s and split between a Dublin collector and an American university, which is why studying it means consulting two catalogues.","Its order of books is its argument. **Hebrews stands second, straight after Romans** — inside the Pauline corpus, not appended at the end as a doubtful case. Whoever made this book had no hesitation about it, at a date when the letter’s authorship was already disputed elsewhere.","And the **Pastoral Epistles are not in it**. Because the codex is a single quire, its total length can be calculated from what survives, and on the standard reconstruction there was never room for 1–2 Timothy and Titus. That is not proof they were rejected — but it is the earliest physical evidence about the shape of the Pauline collection, and the shape is not the one we inherited."],contains:"Romans, Hebrews, 1–2 Corinthians, Ephesians, Galatians, Philippians, Colossians, 1 Thessalonians — with losses at both ends. No Pastorals, and on the reconstruction no room for them.",access:"Digitised by both holding institutions; Kenyon published it in the 1930s.",proves:"That the Pauline collection had a settled shape by about 200 — and that the shape was not quite ours.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Frederic G. Kenyon (ed.), The Chester Beatty Biblical Papyri, Fasciculus III: Pauline Epistles and Revelation (London, 1934–1937)",note:"The editio princeps, published while the leaves were still being divided between Dublin and Michigan."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Chester Beatty Library, Dublin, and P. Mich. inv. 6238, University of Michigan Papyrology Collection — both digitised",note:"The two halves of one book; consulting either alone gives an incomplete quire and a misleading impression of the whole."},{kind:"study",ref:'Jeremy Duff, "P46 and the Pastorals: A Misleading Consensus?" New Testament Studies 44 (1998)',note:"Presses on the quire arithmetic behind the claim that there was no room for the Pastorals — the calculation is not as clean as it is usually reported."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Bruce M. Metzger & Bart D. Ehrman, The Text of the New Testament, 4th ed. (Oxford, 2005)",note:"The standard description, including the position of Hebrews inside the Pauline corpus."}]},{slug:"p75",group:"papyri",siglum:"𝔓⁷⁵",ga:"GA P75",name:"The Bodmer Luke and John",native:"P. Bodmer XIV–XV / Hanna Papyrus 1",date:"c. 175–250 CE",place:"Egypt",script:"Greek majuscule on papyrus, two columns, in a careful and disciplined hand — a professional book, not a private copy",home:"Biblioteca Apostolica Vaticana (transferred 2007)",exposition:["The most consequential manuscript discovery of the twentieth century for the study of the New Testament text, and it settled an argument rather than starting one.","Westcott and Hort had built their 1881 edition on Vaticanus, and the standing objection was that its text might be a **fourth-century recension** — a scholarly clean-up performed in Alexandria, elegant but late, and therefore no closer to the original than anything else. There was no way to test the claim, because nothing older survived.","Then 𝔓⁷⁵ appeared, a century and a half older than Vaticanus, and its text of Luke and John is **extraordinarily close to Vaticanus** — not identical, but far closer than chance or independent development would allow. The Alexandrian text was not invented in the fourth century. It was being copied, carefully, in the second. Gordon Fee’s and Calvin Porter’s analyses in the 1960s made the case, and the modern critical text has rested more comfortably ever since."],contains:"Large parts of Luke and John, consecutive — the earliest manuscript to place two Gospels together in one book.",access:"Given to the Vatican Library in 2007 by the Hanna family; digitised.",proves:"That the shortest, hardest, least harmonised text is not a late scholarly product but an early one — which is why modern Bibles read as they do.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Victor Martin & Rodolphe Kasser (eds.), Papyrus Bodmer XIV–XV: Évangiles de Luc et Jean (Cologny-Genève: Bibliotheca Bodmeriana, 1961)",note:"The editio princeps — the publication that put the argument about a fourth-century Alexandrian recension within reach of evidence."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Vatican City, Biblioteca Apostolica Vaticana, Hanna Papyrus 1 (P. Bodmer XIV–XV), digitised",note:"Where the papyrus has been since 2007, in the same library as the codex it resembles."},{kind:"study",ref:'Calvin L. Porter, "Papyrus Bodmer XV (P75) and the Text of Codex Vaticanus," Journal of Biblical Literature (1962)',note:"The collation that established how close the two texts actually are."},{kind:"study",ref:'Gordon D. Fee, "P75, P66, and Origen: The Myth of Early Textual Recension in Alexandria," repr. in E. J. Epp & G. D. Fee, Studies in the Theory and Method of New Testament Textual Criticism (Grand Rapids: Eerdmans, 1993)',note:"The argument that the Alexandrian text was transmitted rather than manufactured, stated in full."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Bruce M. Metzger & Bart D. Ehrman, The Text of the New Testament, 4th ed. (Oxford, 2005)",note:"The handbook account of why this papyrus changed the standing of Vaticanus."}]},{slug:"vetus-latina",group:"latin",siglum:"a",ga:"VL 3",name:"The Old Latin, and Codex Vercellensis",native:"Vetus Latina",date:"Translations from the 2nd century; Vercellensis c. 350–375 CE",place:"North Africa and Italy; Vercellensis at Vercelli",script:"Latin majuscule; Vercellensis is written in silver on purple-stained parchment for at least part of its extent",home:"Biblioteca Capitolare, Vercelli",exposition:["Before Jerome there was not *a* Latin Bible; there were many, and they disagreed. Augustine complains that in the early days anybody who got hold of a Greek manuscript and thought he knew both languages had a go — and the results were in circulation. Scholars call the whole scatter the **Vetus Latina**, the Old Latin, and it survives in fragments, in quotations from African and Italian bishops, and in a handful of manuscripts.","**Codex Vercellensis**, traditionally ascribed to Eusebius of Vercelli who died in 371, is the oldest of them for the Gospels. It matters out of proportion to its condition, because Old Latin readings are a witness to **Greek manuscripts that no longer exist** — a Latin translator in 200 was working from a Greek copy older than anything now surviving, and where the Latin agrees with Bezae or with a papyrus against the majority, something ancient is showing through.","This is the mess Damasus asked Jerome to clear up, and clearing it up is how the Vulgate began."],contains:"The Gospels, in the order Matthew, John, Luke, Mark, with losses. The Old Latin as a whole is known from perhaps eighty manuscripts and a vast body of patristic quotation.",access:"The Vetus Latina Institute at Beuron has been publishing the reconstructed text book by book since 1949; the work is not finished.",proves:"That translations are witnesses. Where the Greek is lost, its Latin grandchild can still testify to what it said.",sources:[{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Vetus Latina: Die Reste der altlateinischen Bibel, Vetus Latina Institut, Beuron (Freiburg: Herder, from 1949)",note:"The reconstruction of the Old Latin book by book from manuscripts and patristic citation; still unfinished, and the state of completion varies by book."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Bonifatius Fischer, Verzeichnis der Sigel für Handschriften und Kirchenschriftsteller (Vetus Latina 1, Freiburg)",note:"The register behind the lowercase sigla and the VL numbers — where 'a' and 'VL 3' are defined."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Augustine, De doctrina christiana II.11.16",note:"The complaint about anyone with a Greek manuscript and some confidence in two languages producing a version — a contemporary describing the scatter."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Vercelli, Biblioteca Capitolare, Codex Vercellensis (VL 3)",note:"The oldest Old Latin Gospel manuscript, traditionally ascribed to Eusebius of Vercelli; the ascription is tradition, not evidence."},{kind:"study",ref:"H. A. G. Houghton, The Latin New Testament: A Guide to its Early History, Texts, and Manuscripts (Oxford, 2016)",note:"The current guide to the whole Latin line; explains why an Old Latin reading can witness to a lost Greek exemplar."}]},{slug:"vulgate-text",group:"latin",siglum:"vg",ga:"VG",name:"Jerome’s Vulgate",native:"versio vulgata",date:"Gospels revised 383; Old Testament from the Hebrew finished c. 405 CE",place:"Rome, then Bethlehem",script:"Latin — and thereafter the script and the language in which the West did its thinking about scripture for a thousand years",home:"Everywhere. Over ten thousand manuscripts survive, more than for any other text of the Middle Ages.",exposition:["In **382** Pope Damasus asked Jerome to do a limited job: produce one reliable Latin text of the Gospels out of the competing Old Latin versions. Jerome did it, and then kept going — and in going on he made a decision that changed the Western Bible permanently.","For the Old Testament he refused to translate the Greek Septuagint, as every previous Latin version had. He went to the Hebrew, learned it, and translated from what he called the **hebraica veritas**, the Hebrew truth. It took him until about 405. And it meant his Old Testament sometimes said something different from the Bible every Latin-speaking congregation had been hearing read aloud for two centuries.","**Augustine** objected, and the letters between them are the best surviving argument about translation from the ancient world. Augustine’s case was pastoral and not stupid: a bishop in north Africa read Jonah from the new version, the congregation heard an unfamiliar word for the plant that shaded him, and there was a riot. Unity of worship, Augustine argued, is worth more than a philologist’s accuracy. Jerome’s case was that a translation should say what the original says. Jerome won, but it took four hundred years — the Vulgate did not displace the Old Latin until around the ninth century.","It was then declared **authentic** at the Council of Trent in 1546, standardised as the Clementine edition of 1592 after a badly botched Sixtine attempt was withdrawn, and revised as the *Nova Vulgata* in 1979. Its vocabulary is the vocabulary of Western theology: *sacramentum*, *justificatio*, *gratia*, *verbum* — the words the Reformation argued in were Jerome’s."],contains:"The whole Bible. The Gospels are Jerome’s revision of the Old Latin; most of the Old Testament is his fresh translation from Hebrew; some books — Wisdom, Sirach, 1–2 Maccabees, Baruch — are Old Latin that he left alone, and the Psalms circulated in more than one of his own versions at once.",access:"The Stuttgart *Biblia Sacra Vulgata* is the standard critical edition, built on Amiatinus among others; the Clementine text is what most older Catholic Bibles translate.",proves:"That a translation can become the original. For a thousand years, in the Latin West, this *was* the Bible — and every argument of the Reformation was an argument about it.",sources:[{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Biblia Sacra iuxta Vulgatam Versionem, ed. Robert Weber & Roger Gryson (Stuttgart: Deutsche Bibelgesellschaft)",note:"The standard critical edition; its apparatus is where the Vulgate's own variants are, and Amiatinus is among its principal witnesses."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Jerome, Preface to the Gospels (Novum opus), addressed to Damasus; the Prologus Galeatus to Samuel and Kings",note:"Jerome stating his commission and then his own doctrine of the hebraica veritas, in his own defensive prose."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Augustine, Epistulae 28, 71 and 82 (= Jerome, Epistulae 56, 104, 112)",note:"The exchange in full, including the congregation in north Africa that rioted over the plant in Jonah."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Council of Trent, session IV, Decretum de editione et usu sacrorum librorum (1546)",note:"The decree declaring the Vulgate authentic — the text of what was actually said, which is narrower than what is usually reported of it."},{kind:"study",ref:"H. A. G. Houghton, The Latin New Testament (Oxford, 2016)",note:"Traces how slowly the Vulgate displaced the Old Latin, which took centuries rather than a generation."},{kind:"study",ref:"Megan Hale Williams, The Monk and the Book: Jerome and the Making of Christian Scholarship (Chicago, 2006)",note:"On what Jerome was building and why the Hebrew mattered to him more than the peace of the congregations."}]},{slug:"amiatinus",group:"latin",siglum:"Am",ga:"Am",name:"Codex Amiatinus",native:"Amiatino 1",date:"c. 700–716 CE",place:"Wearmouth–Jarrow, Northumbria — the north of England",script:"Latin uncial, two columns, written in sense-lines; 1,030 leaves of vellum, requiring the hides of some five hundred calves",home:"Biblioteca Medicea Laurenziana, Florence",exposition:["The oldest surviving complete Latin Bible in one volume, and the best single witness to Jerome’s text. It weighs about **thirty-four kilograms** — a book two people carry.","It was made in Northumbria, at the twin monastery where **Bede** was then a young monk. Abbot **Ceolfrith** commissioned three complete Bibles: one for each church, and one to be carried to Rome as a gift for the Pope. In 716, aged seventy-four, Ceolfrith set out with it. He died at Langres in Burgundy before reaching Rome. The book went on without him.","And then its origin was lost for eleven hundred years. The dedication page had been altered: Ceolfrith’s name scraped out and replaced so that it read as the gift of a Lombard, and the manuscript was taken for Italian work of the sixth century — the finest Italian book of its age. In **1888** Giovanni Battista de Rossi recovered the original reading, and the greatest Latin Bible in existence turned out to have been made in a monastery on the edge of the North Sea, in a kingdom converted within living memory."],contains:"The entire Vulgate Bible, Old and New Testaments, in a single volume — with the celebrated full-page miniature of Ezra the scribe at his book-cupboard.",access:"In Florence since the eighteenth century, from the abbey of Monte Amiata that gave it its name; digitised, and the base text of the Stuttgart Vulgate.",proves:"That the outermost edge of Latin Christendom could, within eighty years of its own conversion, produce the finest Bible in Europe — and that a scraped-out name can hide a fact for eleven centuries.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Florence, Biblioteca Medicea Laurenziana, Amiatino 1, digitised",note:"Including the dedication page whose scraped and altered lettering hid the book's origin for eleven centuries."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Bede, Historia abbatum; the anonymous Vita Ceolfridi",note:"The near-contemporary notices of Ceolfrith's three pandects and of his departure for Rome in 716."},{kind:"study",ref:"Richard Marsden, The Text of the Old Testament in Anglo-Saxon England (Cambridge, 1995)",note:"Establishes what exemplars Wearmouth–Jarrow was working from, and why the resulting text is so good a witness to Jerome."},{kind:"study",ref:"R. L. S. Bruce-Mitford, The Art of the Codex Amiatinus (Jarrow Lecture, 1967)",note:"On the Ezra miniature and the Mediterranean models behind it — the basis for the long argument over what was copied and what was made."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Biblia Sacra iuxta Vulgatam Versionem, ed. Weber & Gryson (Stuttgart)",note:"Where Amiatinus does its working life, as a base witness in a modern apparatus rather than a museum piece."}]},{slug:"fuldensis",group:"latin",siglum:"F",ga:"Fu",name:"Codex Fuldensis",native:"Codex Bonifatianus I",date:"541–546 CE",place:"Capua, southern Italy",script:"Latin uncial, one column — and corrected in the margins by the bishop who commissioned it, in his own hand",home:"Hessische Landesbibliothek, Fulda",exposition:["Commissioned by **Victor, Bishop of Capua**, who dated his own corrections precisely, so we know the book was finished between 541 and 546. It is a New Testament — and its Gospels are not four Gospels.","Instead it carries a single continuous narrative woven from all four: a Latin form of the **Diatessaron**, the Gospel harmony Tatian made in Syriac around 170. The Syriac original is lost. This Latin book, made in Italy four centuries later, is one of the principal witnesses to what Tatian did — a second-century attempt to solve the problem of four Gospels by having one.","It carries one other reading of consequence. In 1 Corinthians 14, the verses telling women to keep silence in the churches are handled irregularly here, and Fuldensis is among the witnesses cited by scholars who argue those verses were not originally where they now stand. A sixth-century Italian bishop’s copy is part of a live argument about the text of Paul."],contains:"The New Testament entire — with the Gospels replaced by a Latin Diatessaron harmony, and with the Epistle to the Laodiceans included among Paul’s letters.",access:"At Fulda since Boniface owned it in the eighth century; digitised, and edited by Ranke in 1868.",proves:"That the four-Gospel canon had a serious rival for centuries — and that the rival survived in Latin after its Syriac original was destroyed.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Ernst Ranke (ed.), Codex Fuldensis: Novum Testamentum Latine interprete Hieronymo (Marburg and Leipzig, 1868)",note:"The edition; still the standard printed access to the manuscript and to Victor of Capua's dated colophons."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Fulda, Landesbibliothek, Codex Bonifatianus 1, digitised",note:"The book itself, with the bishop's own marginal corrections."},{kind:"study",ref:"William L. Petersen, Tatian's Diatessaron: Its Creation, Dissemination, Significance, and History in Scholarship (Leiden: Brill, 1994)",note:"The standard survey of the harmony and its witnesses; sober about how much of Tatian a sixth-century Latin copy can actually preserve."},{kind:"study",ref:'Philip B. Payne, "Fuldensis, Sigla for Variants in Vaticanus, and 1 Cor 14.34–5," New Testament Studies 41 (1995)',note:"Where this manuscript enters the argument over 1 Corinthians 14:34–35; the reading of its marginal evidence is contested."},{kind:"study",ref:"H. A. G. Houghton, The Latin New Testament (Oxford, 2016)",note:"Places Fuldensis among the Latin witnesses, including the Laodiceans problem."}]},{slug:"sinaitic-syriac",group:"versions",siglum:"syr^s",ga:"Syr. Sin.",name:"The Sinaitic Syriac Palimpsest",native:"ܐܘܢܓܠܝܘܢ ܕܡܦܪܫܐ",lang:"syriac",date:"Gospel text c. 400 CE; overwritten in 778",place:"Written in Syria; found at St Catherine’s, Sinai",script:"Syriac estrangela, one column — scraped away in the eighth century and overwritten with the lives of women saints",home:"St Catherine’s Monastery, Sinai",exposition:["A second palimpsest of the first rank, found at the same monastery as Sinaiticus — and found by two people the field had not expected.","**Agnes Smith Lewis** and **Margaret Dunlop Gibson** were Scottish twin sisters, widowed, wealthy, self-taught in Syriac and Arabic, and refused entry to Cambridge degrees because of their sex. In **1892** they travelled to Sinai, photographed a palimpsest in the monastery library, and recognised beneath the lives of the women saints an Old Syriac text of the four Gospels older than the Peshitta that replaced it.","It is one of only two surviving witnesses to the **Old Syriac** Gospels, and it preserves readings that vanish from every later Syriac Bible — including, at Matthew 1:16, a form of the genealogy whose wording has been argued over ever since. Syriac is Aramaic; these are the Gospels in a sister language of the one Jesus spoke, in a version older than any complete Syriac Bible."],contains:"The four Gospels, with losses, in Old Syriac beneath an eighth-century collection of saints’ lives.",access:"Re-imaged multispectrally in recent decades as part of the Sinai Palimpsests Project, which has recovered far more than the sisters could see.",proves:"That the Peshitta had predecessors — and that in 1892 the two most consequential Syriac scholars in Britain were women the universities would not admit.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"The Four Gospels in Syriac Transcribed from the Sinaitic Palimpsest, ed. R. L. Bensly, J. Rendel Harris & F. C. Burkitt, with an introduction by Agnes Smith Lewis (Cambridge, 1894)",note:"The first transcription, made from the sisters' photographs two years after the discovery."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Agnes Smith Lewis, The Old Syriac Gospels, or Evangelion da-Mepharreshê (London, 1910)",note:"Lewis's own later edition, after further work at the monastery on readings the 1894 team could not settle."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Sinai Palimpsests Project, sinaipalimpsests.org",note:"The multispectral re-imaging of the St Catherine's palimpsests; recovers undertext the sisters had no means of seeing."},{kind:"study",ref:"F. C. Burkitt, Evangelion da-Mepharreshe (Cambridge, 1904)",note:"The Curetonian edition with the Sinaitic collated against it — where the two Old Syriac witnesses are set side by side."},{kind:"study",ref:"Sebastian P. Brock, The Bible in the Syriac Tradition (Piscataway: Gorgias)",note:"The short reliable orientation to the Old Syriac, the Peshitta and what replaced what."},{kind:"study",ref:"Janet Soskice, The Sisters of Sinai: How Two Lady Adventurers Discovered the Hidden Gospels (New York, 2009)",note:"The narrative account of Lewis and Gibson, including the Cambridge that used their finds and would not give them degrees."}]},{slug:"argenteus",group:"versions",siglum:"got",ga:"Codex Argenteus",name:"The Silver Bible",native:"Codex Argenteus",date:"Early 6th century CE",place:"Ravenna, for the Ostrogothic court of Theoderic",script:"Gothic — an alphabet invented for this translation — written in silver and gold ink on parchment stained purple",home:"Uppsala University Library, Sweden",exposition:["In the fourth century the missionary bishop **Wulfila** translated the Bible into Gothic, and to do it he had to invent an alphabet, adapting Greek letters with additions from Latin and the runes. His is the earliest substantial text in any Germanic language — older than any English, German or Norse writing by centuries.","The Codex Argenteus is the finest surviving copy, made for a royal court: silver ink, gold for the sacred names, purple parchment. About half of it survives, containing the four Gospels in the Western order.","Wulfila was an **Arian**, as the Goths were, and his Bible is the scripture of a Christianity that lost. It also, by tradition, omits the books of Kings — Philostorgius reports that he left out the wars of the Israelites on the grounds that the Goths were quite warlike enough already, which if true is the only recorded case of a translator abridging scripture on pastoral grounds.","The book was taken from Prague as war booty by Swedish troops in **1648**, at the very end of the Thirty Years’ War, and has been in Uppsala since. Ten leaves were stolen in 1995 and recovered a month later in a bank deposit box."],contains:"The four Gospels in Gothic, about half surviving, in the order Matthew, John, Luke, Mark.",access:"Digitised in full by Uppsala; a further leaf turned up at Speyer in 1970.",proves:"That a whole literary language can be created to carry a translation — and that the earliest Germanic literature is a Bible belonging to a losing side.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Uppsala University Library, Codex Argenteus — digital facsimile",note:"Silver and gold on purple; the surviving half of the Gothic Gospels as an object."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Codex Argenteus Upsaliensis jussu Senatus Universitatis phototypice editus (Uppsala, 1927)",note:"The photographic edition made for the university's anniversary, and the basis of most subsequent work."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Wilhelm Streitberg, Die gotische Bibel (Heidelberg, 1908)",note:"The standard edition of the Gothic Bible with the reconstructed Greek and a full glossary — the reference for anything said about Wulfila's vocabulary."},{kind:"primary",ref:"Philostorgius, Historia ecclesiastica II.5",note:"The single ancient report that Wulfila omitted Kings because the Goths were warlike enough; one hostile source, and the only one."},{kind:"study",ref:"Carla Falluomini, The Gothic Version of the Gospels and Pauline Epistles (Berlin: De Gruyter, 2015)",note:"The current study of what Greek and Latin texts stand behind the Gothic version."},{kind:"study",ref:"Lars Munkhammar, The Silver Bible: Origins and History of the Codex Argenteus (Uppsala, 2011)",note:"The book's travels — Ravenna, Prague, the 1648 seizure, Uppsala — written from inside the library that holds it."}]},{slug:"garima",group:"versions",siglum:"eth",ga:"Garima I–III",name:"The Garima Gospels",native:"ወንጌል",lang:"geez",date:"Radiocarbon dated c. 390–660 CE",place:"Abba Garima Monastery, Tigray, Ethiopia",script:"Geʼez, in an Ethiopian hand, with full-page illuminations of the evangelists and a domed canon-table building",home:"Abba Garima Monastery, Ethiopia — where they were made, and where they still are",exposition:["Two illuminated Gospel books that have never left the monastery that produced them. Tradition says Abba Garima wrote them in a single day, the sun having been held still for the purpose; radiocarbon dating, published in 2013, puts them between roughly **390 and 660** — which makes them the oldest surviving illuminated Christian manuscripts anywhere, and by a wide margin the oldest complete Geʼez Gospels.","Before the dating they were assumed to be medieval. They are not: they are late antique, and their illuminations — the evangelist portraits, the arched canon tables, the domed structure on the opening pages — are evidence for a Christian book art contemporary with the great uncials, surviving in the one place nobody had looked.","They belong to the church whose canon is the broadest in Christendom, which kept **Enoch** and **Jubilees** when everyone else let them go. They are also still in use, in the building they were made for, which no other manuscript in this section can claim."],contains:"Two complete Gospel books in Geʼez, with a third volume combining leaves from both, and the earliest surviving Christian illumination.",access:"Conserved in situ by the Ethiopian Heritage Fund; the radiocarbon results were published by Jacques Mercier and colleagues.",proves:"That the oldest illuminated Christian book in the world was in Ethiopia the whole time — and that a manuscript can still be doing the job it was made for after sixteen centuries.",sources:[{kind:"primary",ref:"Abba Garima I–III, Abba Garima Monastery, Tigray, Ethiopia",note:"Still in the monastery that made them and still in liturgical use; there is no surrogate collection and access is through the community."},{kind:"study",ref:"Judith S. McKenzie & Francis Watson, The Garima Gospels: Early Illuminated Gospel Books from Ethiopia (Oxford: Manar al-Athar, 2016)",note:"The full study with the photography and the radiocarbon results, and the argument for a late antique rather than medieval date."},{kind:"lexicon",ref:"Rochus Zuurmond, Novum Testamentum Aethiopice: The Synoptic Gospels (Stuttgart: Franz Steiner)",note:"The critical edition of the Ethiopic Gospels; where the Garima text is placed against the later Geʼez tradition."},{kind:"study",ref:"Marilyn E. Heldman, in African Zion: The Sacred Art of Ethiopia (New Haven: Yale, 1993)",note:"The context for the evangelist portraits and canon-table architecture, written before the dating was revised — useful for seeing what the revision overturned."}]}],commentary:[{slug:"reading-a-siglum",heading:"How to read a siglum",body:["Every manuscript in this section carries two names, and the second one is a system.","The **letters** are the older convention, assigned as the great codices came to attention: A for Alexandrinus, B for Vaticanus, C for Ephraemi, D for Bezae, W for Washingtonianus — and ℵ, the Hebrew *aleph*, for Sinaiticus, because Tischendorf wanted the newest and oldest find to stand before A rather than after Z.","The **Gregory–Aland numbers** are the working system, and they encode what kind of thing you are holding. A number preceded by 𝔓 is a papyrus. A number beginning with a zero — 01, 02, 032 — is a majuscule, written in capitals on parchment. A plain number is a minuscule, written in the joined-up hand that took over from the ninth century. A number preceded by ℓ is a lectionary, a book of readings arranged by the church year rather than by book of the Bible.","So 𝔓⁵² and 01 and 2814 are a papyrus scrap, a great uncial, and a twelfth-century minuscule — and knowing that much from the label is the point of the system.","One more convention, easy to miss and worth keeping: in the Latin tradition the sigla are **lowercase**. Codex Vercellensis is *a*, not A — because A was already taken by Alexandrinus, and because the case itself says which tradition you are in. This portal sets its sigla in a font that has both cases, for that reason."]},{slug:"text-types",heading:"What a text-type is, and why the idea is coming apart",body:["For a century the manuscripts were sorted into families. The **Alexandrian** text — Vaticanus, Sinaiticus, 𝔓⁷⁵ — is shorter, rougher, and less harmonised. The **Western** text — Bezae, the Old Latin — is longer, freer, and fond of narrative detail. The **Byzantine** text is fuller and smoother, the product of centuries of careful copying in Constantinople, and it stands behind the vast majority of surviving Greek manuscripts and therefore behind the King James Version.","The scheme did real work. It explained why Alexandrinus behaves one way in the Gospels and another in the Epistles; it let editors weigh witnesses rather than count them, which is the whole methodological advance of the nineteenth century.","It is now under strain. The **Coherence-Based Genealogical Method**, used to produce the *Editio Critica Maior* in Münster, works from the relationships between individual readings rather than from assumed family membership — and what emerges is less like a family tree and more like a network, in which a manuscript can be close kin to another in one verse and distant in the next. “Western” in particular is increasingly regarded as a label for a scatter rather than a group. The families are not abolished; they are being replaced by something with more resolution."]},{slug:"oldest-is-not-best",heading:"Why the oldest manuscript is not automatically the best",body:["The intuition is that an older copy is closer to the original. It is a decent rule of thumb and it is not a principle.","What matters is not the age of the copy but the age of its **exemplar** — the book it was copied from. A fifth-century manuscript copied faithfully from a second-century one is a better witness than a fourth-century manuscript copied from a third-century one that had already been tidied. Age tells you the latest possible date of the text it carries, not the earliest.","This is why 𝔓⁷⁵ mattered so much. It did not overturn Vaticanus; it **confirmed** that Vaticanus was carrying an old text carefully, which is what its defenders had claimed without evidence for eighty years. And it is why the Old Latin is worth reading even in fragments: a translation made in 200 testifies to a Greek manuscript that no longer exists.","The corollary is uncomfortable and worth stating. A reading found in one late manuscript can still be original, and a reading in all the oldest ones can still be a mistake made early. Weight of witness is an argument, not a proof."]},{slug:"some-manuscripts-read",heading:"What “some manuscripts read” is telling you",body:["Open almost any modern Bible at Mark 16, or John 8, or 1 John 5:7, and there will be a bracket, a line, or a footnote. Those marks are the visible end of everything in this section.","The three most consequential cases are worth knowing by name. **Mark 16:9–20**, the resurrection appearances, is absent from Vaticanus and Sinaiticus and present in Alexandrinus, Bezae and Washingtonianus — so the earliest witnesses end at the empty tomb and the fifth century supplies the rest. **John 7:53–8:11**, the woman taken in adultery, is missing from the oldest manuscripts and wanders in the ones that have it, turning up in three different places in John and once in Luke — a true story, most scholars would say, that was looking for a home. And the **Johannine Comma** at 1 John 5:7, the one explicit statement of the Trinity in the New Testament, appears in no Greek manuscript before the fourteenth century; Erasmus left it out of his first two editions, was attacked for it, and put it back.","None of this is hidden. It is printed in the footnotes of the pew Bible, and it is there because the four codices at the top of this page made it impossible to print anything else."]}],outro:["These are not all of them. The Gregory–Aland catalogue currently lists something over five thousand Greek manuscripts of the New Testament alone, and behind them stand the Latin, Syriac, Coptic, Armenian, Georgian, Geʼez, Gothic, Arabic and Slavonic versions, and behind those the patristic quotations — because a bishop citing a verse in 250 is a witness to the text he was reading. What is here is the handful that changed what could be known.","What they established, between Cyril Lucaris’ gift in 1627 and the Bodmer papyri in the 1950s, is that the Bible has a **manuscript history** — not a text handed down entire, but a text reconstructed from witnesses that disagree, by people who have to decide. Every modern translation carries footnotes reading *some manuscripts read*, and these are the manuscripts. The footnote is not an admission of weakness. It is the only honest way to print the thing."]};export{e as default};
